WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.935
The IDE is recreated, but every
Bob reply in the terminal is real:

2
00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:07.960
we ran small probes with IBM Bob Shell on a throwaway copy
of the kit, shortening only task IDs and folder paths.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.003
Where a file sits, and what it says, changes what Bob
does. Every run answers four questions from your files:

4
00:00:13.003 --> 00:00:17.960
who Bob is, what he knows, what he can reach, and what
he may do. We put a probe in most places and watched.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.554
Here are the files that shape a Bob run, and which
question each one answers, as IBM documents them.

6
00:00:24.554 --> 00:00:31.960
The probes that follow test most of this. Files in the
project override the same files in your global Bob folder.

7
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.440
First: who is Bob right now? That's the
mode, defined in custom modes dot yaml.

8
00:01:04.241 --> 00:01:11.710
Second: what does he know? That depends on the root AGENTS
file, the rules and the skills, and on where each one sits.

9
00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:19.884
The root AGENTS.md is already in his context. Our
probe copy's version holds one codeword rule.

10
00:01:19.884 --> 00:01:24.080
Bob replied KIWI-ROOT-AGENTS, with no tool calls.

11
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:31.190
Bob's own /init drafted the kit's version. bob run
doesn't expand /init, so we piped its prompt in.

12
00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:34.190
Bob read the repository and wrote a draft.

13
00:02:55.736 --> 00:03:04.153
Third: what can he reach? The files you
hide from him, and the servers you connect.

14
00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.713
No MCP tool is pre-approved.

15
00:03:16.713 --> 00:03:22.174
alwaysAllow is empty, and the guard hook's code
allows only a short list of read-only MCP tools.

16
00:03:22.214 --> 00:03:32.230
Fourth: what may he do? Past the mode's edit rule, we watched
two layers: the wording of the rules, and the guard hook.

17
00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:35.333
Bob saw through a fake “authorised test”.

18
00:03:35.333 --> 00:03:41.310
He refused and made no tool calls, citing
the rule that a label is not approval.

19
00:04:03.247 --> 00:04:10.993
Before you trust a Bob run, check these.
The ones marked observed, we saw Bob do.

20
00:04:10.993 --> 00:04:14.914
The others come from IBM's documentation.

21
00:04:20.248 --> 00:04:27.368
Try it yourself: put a codeword rule in a
rules folder for your mode, and ask for it.

22
00:04:27.368 --> 00:04:32.226
If the codeword doesn't come back, Bob never saw the rule.
